WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.559 --> 00:00:04.736
- To be a part of something
that people really appreciate,

00:00:04.736 --> 00:00:06.991
as much as maybe certain
people hate to admit it,

00:00:06.991 --> 00:00:08.671
it's important to us.

00:00:08.671 --> 00:00:10.371
Like, it's always, for
me, it's always been

00:00:10.371 --> 00:00:13.271
like, the more simple things
in life that I've appreciated.

00:00:13.271 --> 00:00:15.731
And to be honest with you,
I feel like too much focus

00:00:15.731 --> 00:00:18.004
and I feel like too much
attention is being placed

00:00:18.004 --> 00:00:20.211
on things that really are of no relevance.

00:00:20.211 --> 00:00:22.069
For me, it was really just an idea.

00:00:22.069 --> 00:00:23.980
And as a single person, what's an idea,

00:00:23.980 --> 00:00:25.560
it doesn't really mean anything.

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:27.649
What makes it live and
breathe are the people

00:00:27.649 --> 00:00:30.710
that subscribe to the
philosophy and the idea that--

00:00:30.710 --> 00:00:33.975
- He was an American actor who
began his television career

00:00:33.975 --> 00:00:36.813
guest starring in shows
like Touched By An Angel

00:00:36.813 --> 00:00:38.484
and The Young And The Restless.

00:00:38.484 --> 00:00:41.611
He rose to international fame
for playing Brian O' Connor

00:00:41.611 --> 00:00:44.174
in the hit series Fast And The Furious.

00:00:44.174 --> 00:00:46.806
He also founded the charity
Reach Out Worldwide,

00:00:46.806 --> 00:00:49.573
which was an organization that
helped provide relief efforts

00:00:49.573 --> 00:00:52.102
to countries when they
had natural disasters.

00:00:52.102 --> 00:00:53.861
He's Paul Walker and here's my take

00:00:53.861 --> 00:00:55.914
on his top 10 rules for success.

00:00:55.914 --> 00:00:57.459
Rule number two is my personal favorite,

00:00:57.459 --> 00:00:58.814
I'd make sure to stick around
all the way till the end

00:00:58.814 --> 00:01:00.659
for some special bonus clips.

00:01:00.659 --> 00:01:02.372
Also, as Paul is talking,
if he says something

00:01:02.372 --> 00:01:03.960
that really resonates with you,

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:05.446
please leave it in the comments below

00:01:05.446 --> 00:01:06.706
and put quotes around it

00:01:06.706 --> 00:01:08.810
so other people can be inspired as well.

00:01:08.810 --> 00:01:09.643
Enjoy.

00:01:23.297 --> 00:01:26.393
- I woke up every morning excited.

00:01:26.393 --> 00:01:28.004
I was completely jacked,

00:01:28.004 --> 00:01:29.921
I'd never been more excited
to go to work every day

00:01:29.921 --> 00:01:32.081
as I was on this project.

00:01:32.081 --> 00:01:33.661
At the end of the day,

00:01:33.661 --> 00:01:36.381
I'd never been more drained in my life.

00:01:36.381 --> 00:01:39.143
But somehow, some way, I
found the energy every morning

00:01:39.143 --> 00:01:41.768
to get up and just go for it.

00:01:41.768 --> 00:01:43.491
And the further we got into it,

00:01:43.491 --> 00:01:46.594
the more Wayne was just like
"Okay, do what you want to do,

00:01:46.594 --> 00:01:48.457
"do what you want to do."

00:01:48.457 --> 00:01:51.500
And I never had that
kind of freedom before.

00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:53.131
I enjoy doing them.

00:01:53.131 --> 00:01:56.986
But more than that, actually
let me rephrase that.

00:01:56.986 --> 00:01:59.932
I think the reason why I
enjoy it so much is because

00:01:59.932 --> 00:02:01.340
how much the people get off,

00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:04.327
the audience appreciates what we do.

00:02:04.327 --> 00:02:06.171
There's a lot of things I could be doing

00:02:06.171 --> 00:02:09.745
outside of the industry,
even within the industry.

00:02:09.745 --> 00:02:12.994
But, you know, I don't
know if the satisfaction

00:02:12.994 --> 00:02:15.009
would be the same.

00:02:15.009 --> 00:02:17.023
Because to be a part of something

00:02:17.023 --> 00:02:20.118
that people really appreciate,

00:02:20.118 --> 00:02:22.516
as much as maybe certain
people hate to admit it,

00:02:22.516 --> 00:02:24.966
it's important to us, you know, as humans.

00:02:24.966 --> 00:02:27.694
You want to believe that
what you're putting out there

00:02:27.694 --> 00:02:29.726
and what you've dedicated so much time to,

00:02:29.726 --> 00:02:31.988
there's a certain degree
of acknowledgement.

00:02:31.988 --> 00:02:35.306
And more than acknowledgement,
an appreciation.

00:02:35.306 --> 00:02:38.451
Obviously, this one's been
appreciated for a long time

00:02:38.451 --> 00:02:40.446
and that's a great feeling.

00:02:40.446 --> 00:02:41.769
There really isn't a whole
lot of pressure or stress

00:02:41.769 --> 00:02:43.170
making a movie like this.

00:02:43.170 --> 00:02:46.451
I think the most pressure is
probably felt by the stunt men

00:02:46.451 --> 00:02:48.529
when they got to go do some crazy stunt.

00:02:48.529 --> 00:02:51.083
Because, man, they're putting
their life on the line.

00:02:51.083 --> 00:02:52.144
And there's never a guarantee,

00:02:52.144 --> 00:02:53.946
there's always a degree of risk.

00:02:53.946 --> 00:02:56.636
But for me, it's more
Just about showing up

00:02:56.636 --> 00:03:00.661
and having a good time and
doing the best that I can do.

00:03:00.661 --> 00:03:03.116
But I can really only worry about myself.

00:03:03.116 --> 00:03:06.068
You know, if it doesn't feel
right, it's probably not right

00:03:06.068 --> 00:03:08.286
so let's figure out how we can fix it.

00:03:08.286 --> 00:03:10.306
But I just roll with it.

00:03:10.306 --> 00:03:12.338
One day at a time for me,
that's the way I live my life,

00:03:12.338 --> 00:03:14.379
that's the way I do my job.

00:03:14.379 --> 00:03:18.046
Just come in and I try to
do my best, I give it 110%.

00:03:18.046 --> 00:03:20.611
- Paul, when you first
signed up for the first one,

00:03:20.611 --> 00:03:22.788
which was back in like '99, 2000,

00:03:22.788 --> 00:03:26.206
did you think you'd be
sitting here in 2013

00:03:26.206 --> 00:03:28.042
talking about number six?

00:03:28.042 --> 00:03:29.994
- No, not a chance.

00:03:29.994 --> 00:03:31.894
No screenplay, nothing when I signed on.

00:03:31.894 --> 00:03:33.560
It was just an idea.

00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:35.915
And it's funny because I wanted to do it,

00:03:35.915 --> 00:03:37.064
you know, working with my friends

00:03:37.064 --> 00:03:38.929
'cause I'd already worked
with these people before.

00:03:38.929 --> 00:03:40.183
And they're like "You can't do it."

00:03:40.183 --> 00:03:42.005
My representatives are
telling me "You can't do it,

00:03:42.005 --> 00:03:44.291
"there's no script, there's nothing."

00:03:44.291 --> 00:03:45.467
- You didn't listen to their advice.

00:03:45.467 --> 00:03:47.038
- You were right.
- I got lucky.

00:03:47.038 --> 00:03:48.201
But it's just, you know,
following your heart

00:03:48.201 --> 00:03:49.361
kind of thing.

00:03:49.361 --> 00:03:51.445
And it wasn't even about down the road,

00:03:51.445 --> 00:03:52.854
it was about living in the moment.

00:03:52.854 --> 00:03:54.166
And I got lucky.

00:03:54.166 --> 00:03:58.185
- [Woman] To be somebody
who is a daredevil.

00:03:58.185 --> 00:04:00.769
But in the same time, very private.

00:04:00.769 --> 00:04:04.303
Who doesn't really go
into the Hollywood scene.

00:04:04.303 --> 00:04:08.048
Is there something that
we don't know about you?

00:04:08.048 --> 00:04:10.368
- Well, I think in terms of
just, kind of reservations

00:04:10.368 --> 00:04:13.088
with the whole thing because
I've always considered myself

00:04:13.088 --> 00:04:14.883
to be kind of--

00:04:14.883 --> 00:04:18.730
I don't subscribe to
what's put in our face.

00:04:18.730 --> 00:04:21.589
For me, it's always been the
more simple things in life

00:04:21.589 --> 00:04:23.622
that I've appreciated.

00:04:23.622 --> 00:04:26.377
And to be honest with you,
I feel like too much focus

00:04:26.377 --> 00:04:28.098
and I feel like too much
attention is being placed

00:04:28.098 --> 00:04:30.497
on things that really are of no relevance

00:04:30.497 --> 00:04:33.526
and just really aren't important.

00:04:33.526 --> 00:04:35.777
From the crazy reality
shows and all that stuff

00:04:35.777 --> 00:04:37.260
that we have right now.

00:04:37.260 --> 00:04:38.938
So you know what I'm talking about.

00:04:38.938 --> 00:04:42.646
And so if you're playing in that world

00:04:42.646 --> 00:04:46.081
and you're sensitive to
not wanting to play into it

00:04:46.081 --> 00:04:49.323
to that degree, there's always trepidation

00:04:49.323 --> 00:04:50.995
in every step that you make.

00:04:50.995 --> 00:04:53.857
Because you don't want to be
misinterpreted or misunderstood.

00:04:53.857 --> 00:04:57.196
People are so afraid to
offend or say something.

00:04:57.196 --> 00:05:01.232
It's like maybe if I say
this, I jeopardize my career,

00:05:01.232 --> 00:05:02.824
maybe I won't get another job.

00:05:02.824 --> 00:05:04.679
I don't want to badmouth
this person or that person.

00:05:04.679 --> 00:05:07.721
Personally, I like to be straight up.

00:05:07.721 --> 00:05:11.431
If I had some smack or some
trash to talk to about someone,

00:05:11.431 --> 00:05:13.785
I'd be tempted to give
it to you right now.

00:05:13.785 --> 00:05:15.569
For me, it's really based on,

00:05:15.569 --> 00:05:19.353
my reaction is based on the
general public's reaction.

00:05:19.353 --> 00:05:21.079
I don't know if I like something

00:05:21.079 --> 00:05:23.629
until I know the people like it.

00:05:23.629 --> 00:05:25.785
Especially in movies like
this where the people,

00:05:25.785 --> 00:05:29.390
the fans, they're very
passionate about it.

00:05:29.390 --> 00:05:30.951
The first one was well-received,

00:05:30.951 --> 00:05:32.700
the second one was well-received,

00:05:32.700 --> 00:05:35.311
and now here we are making a fourth one.

00:05:35.311 --> 00:05:37.478
I don't want to disappoint, you know.

00:05:37.478 --> 00:05:38.780
There's a lot of fans out there

00:05:38.780 --> 00:05:41.141
so if they're happy, I'm happy.

00:05:41.141 --> 00:05:43.124
That's pretty much what it comes down to.

00:05:43.124 --> 00:05:45.011
- [Man] How is the relationship
when you're not shooting,

00:05:45.011 --> 00:05:47.668
are you guys like friends?

00:05:47.668 --> 00:05:49.552
- Yeah, we're friends,

00:05:49.552 --> 00:05:52.757
and there's times where
people drive me nuts.

00:05:52.757 --> 00:05:53.780
- [Man] Like in real life?

00:05:53.780 --> 00:05:55.319
- Yeah, in real life.

00:05:55.319 --> 00:05:57.879
It happens, I mean, we've been
working together for so long

00:05:57.879 --> 00:06:00.415
and there's certain
personalities, I'm like "Really,

00:06:00.415 --> 00:06:02.218
"you're still like that guy?"

00:06:02.218 --> 00:06:06.034
But you learn to accept
people for who they are

00:06:06.034 --> 00:06:08.316
and what it's about.

00:06:08.316 --> 00:06:10.246
There's been a lot of self
discovery for all of us

00:06:10.246 --> 00:06:11.246
on this too.

00:06:14.978 --> 00:06:16.448
I love Tyrese like a brother,

00:06:16.448 --> 00:06:18.884
he drives me insane sometimes.

00:06:18.884 --> 00:06:21.853
And we give each other a hard time.

00:06:21.853 --> 00:06:24.770
But there's always love between us.

00:06:25.648 --> 00:06:27.968
Vin, we come from opposite coasts

00:06:27.968 --> 00:06:30.161
and we got two completely
different approaches to life,

00:06:30.161 --> 00:06:31.785
imagine that, you know.

00:06:31.785 --> 00:06:34.229
His childhood was completely
different than mine.

00:06:34.229 --> 00:06:37.232
So sometimes we don't
always see eye to eye.

00:06:37.232 --> 00:06:39.607
But I think we complement one another.

00:06:39.607 --> 00:06:41.404
- [Woman] I understand
you are quite involved

00:06:41.404 --> 00:06:42.947
with your own foundation.

00:06:42.947 --> 00:06:45.623
You are doing some great things for people

00:06:45.623 --> 00:06:47.396
and other people could rally to you

00:06:47.396 --> 00:06:49.635
if they knew something about you.

00:06:49.635 --> 00:06:51.545
- [Woman 2] How come we didn't
know anything about that.

00:06:51.545 --> 00:06:54.550
I just found out you had this foundation.

00:06:54.550 --> 00:06:55.648
- [Paul] About what?

00:06:55.648 --> 00:06:58.281
- [Woman] About you trying to do that.

00:06:58.281 --> 00:07:00.334
- Because it's not about advertising it,

00:07:00.334 --> 00:07:01.620
it's about just doing it.

00:07:01.620 --> 00:07:04.338
The thing that I like is it's not--

00:07:04.338 --> 00:07:07.343
This isn't a promotional campaign.

00:07:07.343 --> 00:07:09.816
This is something where
it was just an idea

00:07:09.816 --> 00:07:13.081
and a few friends went to
Haiti, that's all it was.

00:07:13.081 --> 00:07:15.170
And the Haiti turned into Chile

00:07:15.170 --> 00:07:18.117
and then Chile turned
into, I think, Indonesia.

00:07:18.117 --> 00:07:21.388
And then that turned into, more
recently, the tornado thing.

00:07:21.388 --> 00:07:22.770
And then it was along the way,

00:07:22.770 --> 00:07:26.173
that we realized that we
had something that was good,

00:07:26.173 --> 00:07:28.699
that people actually cared and
were taking an interest in.

00:07:28.699 --> 00:07:31.239
So the idea became a foundation.

00:07:31.239 --> 00:07:34.078
It wasn't like "This is the
idea, let's be a foundation."

00:07:34.078 --> 00:07:35.751
It was just like-minded friends.

00:07:35.751 --> 00:07:38.527
For us, it's a small group
of eight to 12 people.

00:07:38.527 --> 00:07:42.449
We go in, we can make it
happen within 42 to 72 hours.

00:07:42.449 --> 00:07:44.824
And by the time the
Red Cross comes around,

00:07:44.824 --> 00:07:47.667
we can be like "This is
what we figured out."

00:07:47.667 --> 00:07:49.440
And now we leave.

00:07:49.440 --> 00:07:53.085
And I wanted to remain small
and intimate like that.

00:07:53.085 --> 00:07:54.714
It's important, I never wanted--

00:07:54.714 --> 00:07:56.079
Nobody wants it, that's involved,

00:07:56.079 --> 00:07:57.828
wants it to become this big thing.

00:07:57.828 --> 00:08:02.538
Because we will no longer be
effective in our capacity.

00:08:02.538 --> 00:08:04.375
And it's like we're not
going to move mountains

00:08:04.375 --> 00:08:06.082
like the Red Cross does,

00:08:06.082 --> 00:08:08.510
but we're like that
little pebble in the pond.

00:08:08.510 --> 00:08:12.050
We really believe if you can
impact one person's life,

00:08:12.050 --> 00:08:16.068
one family's life, that
radiates from that.

00:08:16.068 --> 00:08:17.089
And so that's the philosophy

00:08:17.089 --> 00:08:18.591
and that's what we're
trying to stick with.

00:08:18.591 --> 00:08:21.148
Yeah, with Reach Out Worldwide,

00:08:21.148 --> 00:08:23.849
things have moved along really well.

00:08:23.849 --> 00:08:26.983
Its inception was last year.

00:08:26.983 --> 00:08:29.552
Last year alone, I think we
had three or four outings.

00:08:29.552 --> 00:08:32.261
We had one this year, we had
the tornado in Tuscaloosa

00:08:32.261 --> 00:08:33.178
in Alabama.

00:08:34.291 --> 00:08:35.942
But this year, for the most part,

00:08:35.942 --> 00:08:38.964
it's been relatively
sleepy, which has been nice.

00:08:38.964 --> 00:08:42.727
But what's happened, there's
been a bit of a ground swell.

00:08:42.727 --> 00:08:44.664
And organically, what's happened

00:08:44.664 --> 00:08:48.786
is we've had different
brands express interest

00:08:48.786 --> 00:08:53.215
in potentially sponsoring us
or helping us with our cause.

00:08:53.215 --> 00:08:55.153
So the thing that's nice is
we put something out there

00:08:55.153 --> 00:08:57.320
that we believed in

00:08:57.320 --> 00:08:59.463
and others have jumped on.

00:08:59.463 --> 00:09:02.922
Davidoff, for instance,
with their donation.

00:09:02.922 --> 00:09:06.796
And since then, STILL, they make chainsaws

00:09:06.796 --> 00:09:09.041
and a lot of power tools.

00:09:09.041 --> 00:09:11.922
And we use a lot of their
equipment in extraction,

00:09:11.922 --> 00:09:13.271
especially in Tuscaloosa.

00:09:13.271 --> 00:09:15.506
You can cut in trees to remove debris

00:09:15.506 --> 00:09:19.316
and help people gain access to their home.

00:09:19.316 --> 00:09:21.726
And it's just exciting,

00:09:21.726 --> 00:09:23.421
it's exciting to see what's happening.

00:09:23.421 --> 00:09:24.881
So the thing that I like is, you know,

00:09:24.881 --> 00:09:27.289
so long as I'm alive and
kicking and I'm making money

00:09:27.289 --> 00:09:30.364
and things are going
well, ROWW can continue.

00:09:30.364 --> 00:09:31.828
But there's going to come a time where

00:09:31.828 --> 00:09:33.844
I might not necessarily be able to fund it

00:09:33.844 --> 00:09:37.443
and so to know that
there's outside interests

00:09:37.443 --> 00:09:38.906
that are coming in now,

00:09:38.906 --> 00:09:40.120
to know that there's a good chance

00:09:40.120 --> 00:09:42.527
that this is something that
could potentially be around

00:09:42.527 --> 00:09:46.997
well after I leave this
planet, it's a good feeling.

00:09:46.997 --> 00:09:50.497
And I think the people that believe in it,

00:09:51.363 --> 00:09:53.499
'cause for me, it was really just an idea,

00:09:53.499 --> 00:09:55.363
and as a single person, what's an idea,

00:09:55.363 --> 00:09:56.765
it doesn't really mean anything.

00:09:56.765 --> 00:09:58.422
What makes it live and breathe

00:09:58.422 --> 00:10:00.767
are the people that
subscribe to the philosophy

00:10:00.767 --> 00:10:03.388
and the idea and the
people that are involved

00:10:03.388 --> 00:10:05.578
have just taken it to a
completely different level

00:10:05.578 --> 00:10:07.639
so I'm really fortunate.

00:10:07.639 --> 00:10:08.940
And the people that are involved

00:10:08.940 --> 00:10:11.273
are just really good people.

00:10:13.041 --> 00:10:14.637
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:10:14.637 --> 00:10:16.935
I made this video because Ngiam Tee Ee,

00:10:16.935 --> 00:10:19.116
I hope I pronounced
that okay, asked me to.

00:10:19.116 --> 00:10:20.402
So if there's a famous entrepreneur

00:10:20.402 --> 00:10:21.856
that you want me to profile next,

00:10:21.856 --> 00:10:23.248
leave it down in the comments below

00:10:23.248 --> 00:10:24.320
and I'll see what I can do.

00:10:24.320 --> 00:10:25.910
I'd also love to know what did Paul say

00:10:25.910 --> 00:10:27.923
that had the biggest impact on you,

00:10:27.923 --> 00:10:29.857
what are you going to
take home from this video

00:10:29.857 --> 00:10:32.169
that you're going to
immediately apply to your life

00:10:32.169 --> 00:10:33.407
or your business.

00:10:33.407 --> 00:10:34.678
Leave it in the comments

00:10:34.678 --> 00:10:36.134
and I'm going to join in the discussion.

00:10:36.134 --> 00:10:37.610
And finally, I wanted to
give a quick shout out

00:10:37.610 --> 00:10:39.944
to Kemo Tedajo, thank you so much

00:10:39.944 --> 00:10:42.446
for buying a copy of
my book, Your One Word,

00:10:42.446 --> 00:10:44.637
and taking that awesome picture with it

00:10:44.637 --> 00:10:46.941
outside the Eiffel Tower in Paris.

00:10:46.941 --> 00:10:49.026
It really really really means a lot to me.

00:10:49.026 --> 00:10:50.475
So thank you guys again for watching.

00:10:50.475 --> 00:10:53.630
I believe in you, I hop you
continue to believe in yourself

00:10:53.630 --> 00:10:55.643
and whatever your one word is.

00:10:55.643 --> 00:10:58.060
Much love, I'll see you soon.

00:11:04.123 --> 00:11:07.873
- Very few of us, myself
included in the few,

00:11:09.033 --> 00:11:12.074
on the early trips, I was
really one of the only ones

00:11:12.074 --> 00:11:15.357
that didn't have any
professional experience.

00:11:15.357 --> 00:11:18.535
And I realized that my
skill set was limited,

00:11:18.535 --> 00:11:19.953
so I didn't like that feeling.

00:11:19.953 --> 00:11:22.424
As much as I picked up while
we were there and in the field,

00:11:22.424 --> 00:11:24.645
I realized that there was more to learn.

00:11:24.645 --> 00:11:27.697
And that's a big part of it
that really keeps me drawn

00:11:27.697 --> 00:11:30.060
is like, we're only here one time.

00:11:30.060 --> 00:11:33.204
And I love the arena, I love
learning about first aid

00:11:33.204 --> 00:11:35.610
and anything pertaining to medicine,

00:11:35.610 --> 00:11:37.098
it's fascinating to me

00:11:37.098 --> 00:11:39.553
so it's one thing I continue to work on

00:11:39.553 --> 00:11:42.239
and work towards and work forward with.

00:11:42.239 --> 00:11:45.519
And hopefully, within not too much longer,

00:11:45.519 --> 00:11:48.054
I'll feel qualified, super qualified.

00:11:48.054 --> 00:11:50.690
- [Woman] Who's the
most badass guy on set?

00:11:50.690 --> 00:11:52.472
Who's the most, you know?

00:11:52.472 --> 00:11:54.717
- Dwayne's the biggest guy.
- Yeah?

00:11:54.717 --> 00:11:56.967
- And he's pretty athletic.

00:11:58.388 --> 00:12:00.663
I can do the most.

00:12:00.663 --> 00:12:03.452
I can surf and I can skate
and I can play volleyball

00:12:03.452 --> 00:12:05.871
and do all that stuff,
those guys can't do that.

00:12:05.871 --> 00:12:09.871
So it's all a matter of
what you value, I guess.

00:12:10.737 --> 00:12:14.114
I race cars, I can drive
better than everybody else.

00:12:14.114 --> 00:12:15.364
- [Tyrese] Vin!

00:12:16.323 --> 00:12:17.750
Where is he?

00:12:17.750 --> 00:12:18.583
Vin!

00:12:20.639 --> 00:12:21.472
Vin!

00:12:23.464 --> 00:12:25.429
Everybody's looking for
you in the trailer, man,

00:12:25.429 --> 00:12:28.012
I just called your phone like--

00:12:34.361 --> 00:12:37.787
Paul, what the fuck are you doing, man?

00:12:37.787 --> 00:12:39.972
Dog, what are you doing?

00:12:39.972 --> 00:12:41.389
- I'm Vin Diesel.

00:12:43.788 --> 00:12:46.021
- Paul, look, if he
see you like this, dog,

00:12:46.021 --> 00:12:49.177
he's going to flip out, dog,
you're doing too much right now.

00:12:49.177 --> 00:12:50.874
- Diesel time.

00:12:50.874 --> 00:12:52.500
Diesel time!

00:12:52.500 --> 00:12:53.938
Diesel!

00:12:53.938 --> 00:12:55.805
What time is it?

00:12:55.805 --> 00:12:57.222
It's Diesel time!

00:12:58.063 --> 00:12:59.063
Diesel time!

00:13:04.254 --> 00:13:05.337
That's right.

00:13:06.380 --> 00:13:07.953
Diesel time, bitches.

00:13:07.953 --> 00:13:11.703
- Back in 2002, it was
you, the MTV audience,

00:13:13.878 --> 00:13:17.206
that first acknowledged this brotherhood

00:13:17.206 --> 00:13:19.706
between Paul Walker and myself

00:13:20.964 --> 00:13:24.464
by honoring us with the best team on film.

00:13:26.934 --> 00:13:31.101
So it's surreal to be
standing here 12 years later

00:13:33.559 --> 00:13:36.420
and that you have been so generous

00:13:36.420 --> 00:13:38.753
to give us this award again.

00:13:47.163 --> 00:13:50.163
I wish he could be here to see this.

00:13:52.400 --> 00:13:54.983
But I know he's here in spirit.

00:13:57.454 --> 00:13:59.537
Thank you for honoring me

00:14:01.668 --> 00:14:05.835
and more importantly, thank
you for honoring that angel,

00:14:06.749 --> 00:14:08.749
my brother, Paul Walker.

